{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>For thus says YHWH.</b> The captives that the mighty one took shall be taken from him." "<b>Tyrant.</b> Like 'mighty one,' and it is an epithet of the noun, like the word אימים (Genesis 14:5); behold, it is in construct state." "<b>And moreover [your contender I will contend with].</b> For I will make a contention with the men of your contention. Many said that יריבך has its root from possessors of yod in the first [position]; others said it is inverted [hollow]. The Jerusalem [Targum] says that the word אשר is missing, as though it said ועם אשר יריבך, and it places the yod, which is for the future. Now, did he forget ריבה יהוה את יריבי (Psalms 35:1)? The upshot is that the yod is a strange letter."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {2 "The phrase 'inverted [hollow]' renders הפוך, Ibn Ezra's term for a root whose middle letter is weak; 'possessors of yod in the first [position]' renders בעלי היו\"ד בראשונה. The bracketed Hebrew [יריבך] in the second clause appears to be supplied by the editor."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:58:53.682514164Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}